  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe center of Tropical Storm Bertha is currently located inland near \nthe Texas and Louisiana border, however the rain is displaced well \nto the southwest of the center due to mid/upper level shear. As TS \nBertha continues a westward movement, showers and thunderstorms \ncurrently over the northwestern gulf will move inland later this \nafternoon along the TX coast. Medium to high rain chances expected \nacross the Victoria Crossroads and Coastal Bend late afternoon into \ntonight. The higher rain chances shift west across the Brush Country \nand Rio Grande Plains overnight with rain chances ending from east \nto west by Friday morning. Rainfall amounts of 1 to 3 inches with \nisolated higher amounts are expected generally south and east of a \nline from Victoria to Laredo. Localized flooding is possible with \nthe stronger storms with high rainfall rates. At this time, \nwidespread flooding is not anticipated. As for strong winds, the \nstronger storms may produce wind gusts up to 40-50 mph. Isolated \nsevere storms are possible with damaging wind being the main threat.\n\nHeat remains the other concern through this afternoon with a Heat \nAdvisory in effect until 7PM this evening. At this time, heat \nadvisory conditions are not anticipated through the weekend with \nheat indices generally 100-109. A moderate risk of heat related \nimpacts can be expected Friday through the weekend with an \nincreasing risk to major impacts next week as a mid level high \npressure system centered over the U.S., maintains influence over S \nTX. Very low to nil rainfall chances expected this weekend into next \nweek.",
